"""Actually completed whole-field source readings, frozen before candidate construction."""
NOTES = {
 ('abudawud:2705','hi'): "Read the whole Hindi. An unnamed Ansari narrates the journey, severe need, taking goats, boiling pots, the Messenger's arrival leaning on his bow and overturning the pots/meat. The ending expressly supplies two alternative orderings of the carrion/loot comparison; retain both actual alternatives in the same account without choosing one or naming the anonymous reporter. No detached Hindi compiler tail is printed.",
 ('abudawud:2705','ur'): "Read the complete Urdu independently. The unnamed Ansari's actual journey/need/goats/pots/bow account includes the two alternative comparison clauses. Preserve both as transmitted uncertainty, not two incidents. The final sentence identifying Hannad as the source of doubt is transmission apparatus and the numbered footnote is context; the source criticism remains visible without deleting either finite alternative from the account.",
 ('abudawud:2707','hi'): "Read the whole Hindi. Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghanm first narrates his siege with the source-named Sharjil, capture and division of goats/cattle at Qinnasrin; he then meets Muadh and describes this to him. Muadh replies with a distinct Khaybar participation/distribution account involving the Messenger. Preserve the first narrator's actual event and the bounded embedded Companion report as separately voiced components/families, not one Prophetic eyewitness scene. Retain every distribution alternative and the source names without external repair.",
 ('abudawud:2707','ur'): "The complete Urdu independently gives Ibn Ghanm's Qinnasrin siege/distribution, subsequent meeting with Muadh, and Muadh's separate Khaybar report. Keep the first-person historical scene under Ibn Ghanm and the embedded Messenger-related distribution under Muadh, with their distinct event families within this one source occurrence. The final numbered footnote is apparatus; no source quantity or participant is filled from outside.",
 ('abudawud:2720','hi'): "The entire short Hindi is only a through-this-chain/same-way transmission pointer naming Awf ibn Malik. It supplies no report body, event, predicate of the underlying report or quoted saying. Preserve the named chain and all wording as source context; the other three language fields must also be read before any whole-parent exclusion.",
 ('abudawud:2720','ur'): "Read every Urdu word. The field only says that the same has been transmitted from Awf through this chain. The transmission pointer itself supplies no substantive matn. Retain it as source context; a source-only parent requires complete inspection of the other original fields, not inference from this translation.",
 ('abudawud:2720','ar'): "Read the complete Arabic chain. Ahmad ibn Hanbal, al-Walid's asking Thawr about this hadith, Khalid ibn Madan, Jubayr ibn Nufayr, his father and Awf form a transmission route ending نحوه. The finite asking/relating verbs are part of that prefatory transmission machinery, with no actual body of the referenced report. Preserve the entire source as context and do not fabricate matn or promote the route discussion into a new incident.",
 ('abudawud:2720','en'): "Read the complete English. It states only that the previously mentioned tradition was also transmitted by Awf through a different narrator chain. No own report body is supplied. Preserve this transmission statement as source context and do not import the previous tradition.",
 ('abudawud:2720','ur-Latn'): "Read the full Roman Urdu. It is the same bare through-this-chain/from-Awf/similarly-transmitted pointer, with no substantive body. All five own fields have now been completely read and are source apparatus; the parent can be resolved source-only without creating a narration or missing identity.",
 ('abudawud:2722','hi'): "Read the whole Hindi. The source-named Abdullah bin Masud reports the Messenger giving him Abu Jahl's sword as nafl on Badr day. The final third-person clause states that he had killed him, but its printed pronouns are not externally resolved or silently recast as first person. Preserve that finite appended clause with qualified attribution in the same account; retain the Badr day, sword object and source name defect exactly.",
 ('abudawud:2722','ur'): "Read the complete Urdu independently. Abdullah ibn Masud's actual first-person sword-allocation report is followed by a third-person killing clause. Keep both supplied clauses and the Badr/nafl scope, leaving the final pronoun relation source-qualified rather than deciding it from another edition. The last numbered footnote is context and all actual source wording remains unchanged.",
 ('abudawud:2725','hi'): "Read the complete Hindi. Abu Musa recounts coming from the explicitly supplied Habasha origin and meeting at Khaybar's conquest, allocation from the explicitly named spoils, an alternative allocated/gave wording and the absence/participation restriction with the ship-party exception identifying Jafar and companions. Both parenthetical origin and property-source qualifiers are material narrative scope and remain. Preserve the awkward exception/negative sequence and printed alternative without harmonization or new identity inference.",
 ('abudawud:2725','ur'): "The entire Urdu independently provides Abu Musa's arrival from Habasha, meeting at Khaybar, spoil allocation/giving alternative, attendance restriction and ship-group/Jafar exception. The parenthetical origin and spoils source materially restrict the account and remain qualified source rendering. Preserve the exact negative/exception wording; only the final numbered footnote is apparatus, with no missing battle participation supplied.",
 ('abudawud:2729','hi'): "Read every Hindi word. Hashraj's grandmother, explicitly identified in the source parenthesis as Umm Ziyad al-Ashjaiyyah, supplies the six-women expedition, summons, displeasure, actual permission questions, their services and reply, and allocation at Khaybar. Keep the supplied grandmother identity as material speaker attribution, not an inferred biography. The concluding Hashraj-to-grandmother question about what the share was and her dates answer is a finite later reception dialogue, separately voiced from her earlier expedition account; no blanket compiler-tail exclusion applies.",
 ('abudawud:2729','ur'): "Read the whole Urdu independently. The printed grandmother identity and six-woman count are part of the attributed expedition account, with the actual summons/questions, women's explanation, go-ahead and later distribution. Hashraj then expressly asks his grandmother about the share and receives the dates reply. Preserve that bounded later question/answer as a separate reception component/family, not as the Prophet's words or an independent claim of equal monetary shares. The final footnote glyph remains context.",
 ('abudawud:2732','hi'): "Read the complete Hindi. Aisha reports an unnamed polytheist's arrival with the stated aim of fighting alongside the Messenger and the actual return/no-help reply. Preserve the participant class, purpose and reply under this bounded narrated event; no external identity, campaign or later rule is supplied. The short Aisha attribution and entire finite field remain.",
 ('abudawud:2732','ur'): "The complete Urdu independently supplies the unnamed participant, his aim of joining the fight and the reported reply. Keep the source's actual event and negative scope through Aisha without inventing the campaign or harmonizing with other reports. There is no detached apparatus to select as another report.",
 ('abudawud:2734','hi'): "Read all Hindi. The source's awkward narrator genealogy says Abu Umra's father Abd al-Rahman bin Abi Umra Ansari; preserve that exact named/relational attribution without repair. The actual account has four men, one horse, one share per man and two for the horse. Retain all quantities and distribution recipients under the source account.",
 ('abudawud:2734','ur'): "Read every Urdu word. The printed narrator relationship is potentially awkward but remains unchanged and source-qualified. The four-men/one-horse encounter and one-each/two-horse allocation are complete supplied content. No participant identity or number is normalized using another version.",
 ('abudawud:2741','hi'): "Read the full Hindi. Abdullah bin Umar reports the Najd army dispatch, a detachment and twelve-each plus an extra camel leading to thirteen. The Hindi loses part of the army-recipient/conjunction link before the twelve quantity; keep this defective finite sequence as printed and do not import Urdu's explicit لشکر کے لوگوں phrase. Preserve both group references and all stated counts.",
 ('abudawud:2741','ur'): "The entire Urdu independently gives the Najd army dispatch, a detachment sent against the enemy, twelve camels for the army members and the detachment's extra one yielding thirteen. Preserve the source's group distinction, arithmetic and dispatch purpose under Ibn Umar's report. These clearer Urdu links must not repair the shorter Hindi.",
 ('abudawud:2743','hi'): "Read the full Hindi. After the bare through-this-chain preface, Ibn Umar supplies his Najd expedition participation, camels, the commander's one-each bonus, later return and the Messenger's twelve-each distribution after khums. The earlier bonus was neither counted nor reproached and the final total is thirteen. Preserve this complete finite sequence and every negative/quantity, without reconciling the bonus-giver with other occurrences. Keep only the initial route pointer as context.",
 ('abudawud:2743','ur'): "Read the whole Urdu independently. The same-route preface is apparatus, but Ibn Umar's actual expedition and allocation narrative is fully supplied. Preserve the commander bonus, later distribution after khums, non-counting/non-reproach and thirteen total; the body footnote marker is context. The source's actual giver and sequence must not be normalized against2745.",
 ('abudawud:2745','hi'): "Read every Hindi word. The main Ibn Umar report explicitly attributes the dispatch, twelve-camel share and one-camel bonus to the Messenger. Abu Dawud's subsequent route-equivalence discussion is transmission apparatus, but it contains a finite alternative passive wording that we were each given one camel as nafl. Preserve that exact actual variant as a separate transmitted-variant component in the same report family with its giver unspecified; the note that this version does not mention the Prophet is context and must prevent an invented Prophetic agent. The bare opening same-chain phrase is also context.",
 ('abudawud:2745','ur'): "The entire Urdu supplies a main Messenger-giver account and, inside the later version discussion, the actual passive clause that we were each given a camel as nafl. Keep both supplied bodies with different attribution limits and one source event family. The named routes, same-as labels and omitted-Prophet commentary are context; do not fill the variant's giver from the main body or turn the route discussion into a new report.",
 ('abudawud:2755','hi'): "Read the whole Hindi. Amr ibn Abasa narrates the prayer facing a spoils camel, completion with salutation, taking a hair from the camel's side and the actual limited-rights statement with its khums exception and return clause. Preserve the complete action sequence, tiny-quantity comparison and exception under the source's attribution; no body or lexical apparatus is omitted in this field.",
 ('abudawud:2755','ur'): "Read every Urdu word. The prayer/camel/hair scene and both limbs of the reported khums statement are fully supplied. Retain the facing direction, after-salam sequence, hair-side detail and exception/return scope. The final numbered footnote alone is context; no independent property ruling or missing allocation is supplied.",
 ('abudawud:2775','hi'): "Read the complete Hindi. The source names only Saad, who reports travel from Mecca toward Medina, the printed Azwara location and repeated raised-hand supplication/prostration. The Messenger then gives the actual first-person explanation of three thirds and thankful prostrations. Preserve that narration and nested explanation in their source order, all quantities and the printed रिक्वेस्ट word, without filling a fuller Saad identity or expanding the shorter Hindi action details from Urdu.",
 ('abudawud:2775','ur'): "Read the entire Urdu independently. Saad's travel account contains the own repeated standing, raised hands, duration and prostration wording, followed by the reported three-thirds explanation. Preserve every actual temporal/quantity detail and the nested Messenger voice as supplied; the location footnote glyph alone stays context. Do not harmonize the more detailed Urdu with Hindi or make the reported explanation an independently certified claim.",
 ('tirmidhi:2711','hi'): "Read all Hindi. Jabir's actual visit-permission request concerns his father's debt; the ensuing who-is-it, I and repeated-I exchange is supplied. The parenthetic '(क्या है?)' renders a finite questioning predicate in the reply rather than a detached word synonym, and remains qualified source-rendered speech. Preserve the debt relationship, the actual response and its repetition; the appended Tirmidhi grade is context.",
 ('tirmidhi:2711','ur'): "Read the entire Urdu independently. Jabir's request, father-debt object and actual door/identity exchange remain, including the parenthetic questioning predicate as this source's rendered reply scope. Do not remove it merely for parentheses or assert historical verbatim equivalence. The numbered footnote and final grading sentence are apparatus; all actual source punctuation stays.",
 ('tirmidhi:2715','hi'): "Read the whole Hindi. Zayd narrates the instruction to learn Jewish writing, the actual oath/explanation, learning within less than half a month and his later writing/reading service. The parenthetic तहरीर repeats the writing sense of the already contextual किताब and is lexical context. After grading and alternate-route apparatus, the source prints another finite Zayd report that the Messenger ordered Syriac learning; preserve that own actual variant separately within the same commission family, without replacing the initial wording or making it another witnessed event.",
 ('tirmidhi:2715','ur'): "The full Urdu independently supplies the learning commission, stated reason, less-than-half-month interval and subsequent letter service. Keep those acts and quotation unchanged. The dictionary parenthesis for کتاب and the footnote/grade/alternate-chain list remain context. The final named Zayd variant actually supplies a Syriac-learning command; retain the complete finite clause with qualified transmitted-variant attribution, rather than discarding the whole tail or harmonizing its language with the opening.",
 ('tirmidhi:2717','hi'): "Read every Hindi word. Ibn Abbas relays Abu Sufyan's own merchant-group presence in Syria, Heraclius's summons, their arrival and the later narrated reading of the Messenger's letter. Preserve the explicitly quoted letter excerpt and its supplied translation, including the actual sender/recipient titles and greeting; no omitted remainder is supplied. The parenthetic Syrian-emperor title is a background apposition to the already named actor, and the parenthetic Rahman/Rahim synonyms are lexical notes; keep those as context while retaining actual letter wording and rendering. The final ellipsis/etc marker, grading and Abu Sufyan name biography are apparatus. The source's brief continued/said frame is preserved with the relayed account and not treated as new quoted letter text.",
 ('tirmidhi:2717','ur'): "Read the complete independent Urdu. Preserve Ibn Abbas's explicit relay of Abu Sufyan's encounter and the letter-reading event, followed by the actual Arabic letter excerpt and Urdu rendering. The Heraclius background-title parenthesis and the two divine-name synonym parentheses are explanatory context; the title عظيم الروم inside the actual letter and its translation remain supplied quotation. Keep the finite reporter transition and all own source name/punctuation defects, with the omitted-remainder marker and compiler grade/genealogy left outside. No body continuation or historical identity is imported.",
 ('tirmidhi:2718','hi'): "Read the full Hindi. Anas reports the letter-sending intention, the information that unsealed letters are not accepted, the ring-making and his own printed palm/shine observation. The kings parenthesis materially restricts the intended recipients, and for-seal supplies the ring's actual purpose; retain both as source-rendered scope. Preserve the damaged Hindi observation join without adding an absent as-if predicate. The appended grading alone is context.",
 ('tirmidhi:2718','ur'): "Read all Urdu independently. Anas's source account supplies intended foreign kings, their seal requirement, a ring made for that purpose and the actual shine/palm observation. Retain the recipient and purpose parentheses because they materially specify the event rather than merely define a word. Keep the defective final link as printed and the observation under Anas; the Tirmidhi grade is context.",
 ('tirmidhi:2720','hi'): "Read the complete Hindi. Ibn Umar reports an unnamed man's greeting during the Messenger's urination and the non-response. Keep the precise temporal circumstance, unnamed participant and negative reply fact under this single event; no general rule or missing response is supplied. The short reporting frame is coherent body attribution.",
 ('tirmidhi:2720','ur'): "Read all Urdu independently. The source supplies the greeting, the urination circumstance and the actual lack of response. Preserve those limited facts under Ibn Umar rather than infer a broader rule or identify the greeter. The whole own field is a finite report without a detached tail.",
 ('tirmidhi:2722','hi'): "Read every Hindi word. Jabir ibn Sulaym narrates his arrival, the actual alayka-salam greeting and its parenthetical source translation, then the reported correction to as-salamu-alaykum. Preserve both actual quoted forms and the supplied rendering as the contents of this exchange. The forward long-story pointer and grade are context, with no continuation reconstructed. Hindi's plural form is not replaced by Urdu's printed singular form.",
 ('tirmidhi:2722','ur'): "The whole Urdu independently gives arrival, علیک السلام with its supplied meaning, and the actual corrected السلام علیک form. Preserve that own singular spelling without harmonizing Hindi or an external familiar formula. The actual greeting translation is content of the exchange; the long-story pointer and compiler grade remain context and do not supply the omitted story.",
 ('tirmidhi:2725','hi'): "Read the complete Hindi. Jabir ibn Samurah reports the group's recurring practice of sitting wherever space was available when they came to the Prophet. Preserve it as the Companion/group observation, not a direct Prophetic instruction. The appended grading sentence is context; no location or seating rule is expanded from elsewhere.",
 ('tirmidhi:2725','ur'): "Read every Urdu word. The group-arrival/seating observation is actual body under Jabir ibn Samurah. The final footnote, numbered grade and alternative Zuhayr/Samak route are apparatus without additional supplied report content. Keep the actual where-space-was-available condition and no invented command.",
 ('tirmidhi:2728','hi'): "Read the full Hindi. Anas narrates an unnamed man's three meeting/greeting questions and the two negative replies followed by yes to the hand-taking question. Preserve the own brother-or-friend alternatives and the defective Hindi बॉस and मुस्तफ़ा words without repairing them from Urdu. The final parenthetic only-this-is-enough is a finite sufficiency restriction rendered within the reply and remains explicitly source-qualified rather than claimed as independently verified verbatim speech. The final grade is context.",
 ('tirmidhi:2728','ur'): "The whole Urdu independently supplies the three questions about bowing, embracing/kissing and taking the hand/shaking, with two no replies and a final yes. Keep the actual alternatives, all questions and replies, and the printed sufficiency predicate in the final answer as qualified source rendering. The footnote and compiler grade remain context. Do not transfer the clearer Urdu action words into damaged Hindi.",
 ('tirmidhi:2732','hi'): "Read the complete Hindi. Aisha narrates Zayd's arrival, the Messenger being at her house at that time, knocking, his movement gathering clothes, her own never-before/after observation and the embrace/kiss. The at-that-time parenthesis supplies material simultaneity and the advancing parenthesis an actual movement; retain both as this source's rendering. Keep Aisha's oath/observation distinct from the Messenger's acts, with the compiler grade/route restriction as context.",
 ('tirmidhi:2732','ur'): "Read every Urdu word independently. The source gives the actual arrival/house/knocking scene, clothes/movement, Aisha's stated observation and the embrace/kiss. Retain the two temporal/action parentheses as finite narrative scope. The body footnote and final grade/single-route appraisal remain source apparatus, without normalizing the source's bodily description or inventing omitted circumstances.",
 ('tirmidhi:2733','hi'): "Read the whole Hindi. Safwan narrates the two Jewish interlocutors' private exchange, their visit and nine-sign question, the Messenger's supplied answer, their kiss/testimony, his follow-up and their David-prayer/fear explanation. Preserve these actual distinct voices within one encounter, including the source-rendered Musa identification of the signs and the hearing/response transition. The nine parenthetic enumeration labels are apparatus, not extra quoted numerals; the Saturday synonym and following/ittiba synonym are lexical context. The actual nine quantity, numbered instruction bodies, Sabbath word, conditions and alleged David explanation remain under their printed speakers; the final grade/cross-report list is context.",
 ('tirmidhi:2733','ur'): "Read the complete independent Urdu. The actual encounter has Safwan's reporting frame, two unnamed interlocutors, the finite nine-sign question, the Messenger's answer and follow-up, then their own claimed David-prayer rationale and fear. Do not flatten the interlocutors' assertions into Prophetic speech. Retain the Musa-object and heard-your-answer qualifications as source-rendered material scope. Keep each actual instruction while separating the nine displayed enumeration markers, Saturday/following lexical synonyms, and final compiler grading/cross-report apparatus. No scripture or missing identity is supplied.",
 ('tirmidhi:2734','hi'): "Read all Hindi. Umm Hani reports coming in the conquest year, bathing, Fatima's cloth screen, her greeting and identity response, and the Messenger's welcome. Preserve the entire finite encounter and its actual participant/time details. The later narrator assertion that Abu Murra told the full incident is an omitted-remainder pointer, followed by a grade; both remain context, and no missing incident body is imported.",
 ('tirmidhi:2734','ur'): "The complete Urdu independently supplies Umm Hani's conquest-year visit, the bath/screen scene and exact greeting/identity/welcome dialogue. Keep these supplied words and own voices. The Abu Murra full-story marker and final grading are apparatus without extra body, so do not manufacture the continuation from another occurrence.",
 ('tirmidhi:2738','hi'): "Read the full Hindi including the literal &quot; entity text and spelling defects. Nafi narrates the unnamed sneezer's formula and its supplied translation, then Ibn Umar's actual response contrasting what he too could say with what the Messenger taught. Retain the quoted formulas and complete source renderings, even where introduced by yani, because they are the narrated utterance contents rather than a disconnected lexicon. Distinguish Nafi's scene, the sneezer and Ibn Umar's reported reply; only the compiler grade is context, and escaped entities are not decoded or repaired.",
 ('tirmidhi:2738','ur'): "Read every Urdu word independently. The sneezer's supplied formula/rendering and Ibn Umar's finite response remain with their actual speakers, including the negative taught-us claim and the different taught formula. The source's whole-utterance translations remain content; the two numbered note glyphs and final grade/single-transmitter statement are context. Do not recast all of Ibn Umar's reply as a direct Prophet quotation or copy Urdu's clean script into Hindi's entity text.",
 ('tirmidhi:2739','hi'): "Read the whole Hindi. Abu Musa reports the Jewish participants' hopeful sneezing and expected mercy formula, then the actual different guidance/welfare response. Preserve the distinction between hoped-for and actually spoken wording and both supplied translations. The parenthetic on-this-occasion-only materially limits the actual response domain and remains source-qualified. The final grade and other-report list are apparatus, not additional admitted sayings.",
 ('tirmidhi:2739','ur'): "Read all Urdu independently. The source explicitly contrasts the formula hoped for with the one actually said. Keep both quoted formulas and their translations under that contrast, plus the parenthetic occasion/only restriction as material source-rendered scope. The body footnote and compiler grading/cross-report names stay context; no general or externally authenticated rule is inserted.",
 ('tirmidhi:2743.4','hi'): "Read the complete Hindi, preserving the exact decimal source number2743.4. The opening route/equivalence labels and final corroborating-chain parenthesis are apparatus, but the intervening variant actually says that the honorific-marked आप told an unnamed person on the third occasion that he had a cold. Retain that finite temporal reply as an explicitly transmitted variant, with the source's honorific-framed speaker rather than an externally supplied personal name or missing earlier exchange. No full parent story is reconstructed.",
 ('tirmidhi:2743.4','ur'): "The entire Urdu independently contains an actual third-time reply after the version comparison. Preserve the exact honorific-marked آپ frame, third occasion and cold statement, with the recipient unnamed. The prefatory Abd al-Rahman/Ikrima/Ibn-Mubarak comparison and final supportive-chain note remain context. The decimal identifier remains source-local; no missing sneeze narrative is filled.",
 ('tirmidhi:2748','hi'): "Read all Hindi. The source identifies the reporter only as Thabit's father and explicitly gives a Prophet-said frame for the list within prayer. Retain that own reported saying, prayer domain and all listed items without importing an ultimate name. The appended grade, route limitation and Tirmidhi/Bukhari/Yahya narrator-name discussion are transmission/biographical apparatus, despite their question/answer verbs; they do not become new matn or fill the source reporter's identity.",
 ('tirmidhi:2748','ur'): "Read every Urdu word independently. The actual opening has Thabit's father, the stated Prophet attribution and the full prayer-context list. Preserve it as a historical attributed report, not a present medical explanation. The whole compiler grading, route criticism and questions about Adi's grandfather belong to source apparatus; the name Dinar is not transferred into the admitted speaker from that discussion.",
 ('tirmidhi:2753','hi'): "Read the complete Hindi. Abu Mijla's source reports a man sitting in the circle and then a statement expressly presented as according to Muhammad, but the intervening Hudhayfa speaker/link printed in Urdu is absent. Preserve the finite sitting event and the actual source-attributed statement with this missing link explicitly qualified; do not fill the intermediary. The circle synonym parenthesis is lexical context and the final grade is apparatus. The defective Hindi narrator spelling remains unchanged.",
 ('tirmidhi:2753','ur'): "The whole Urdu independently names Abu Mijlaz for the sitting event and Hudhayfa for the subsequent statement relaying Muhammad's words. Preserve those distinct supplied voices and the actual sitting-among-seated-people domain. The circle/daira synonym, footnote, grade and narrator-name biography are context, without copying Hudhayfa or the biographical fuller name into Hindi.",
 ('tirmidhi:2759','hi'): "Read the complete Hindi. Anas states in passive form that the limit for moustache, nails, lower-abdominal hair and armpit hair has been set for us, followed by the no-more-than-forty-days qualification. The prescribing agent is not explicitly named in this own field; retain a Companion report with that attribution gap, not an invented direct Prophet quote. Keep all four acts and the exact limit. The final comparative grading is context.",
 ('tirmidhi:2759','ur'): "Read all Urdu independently. Preserve Anas's passive prescribed-for-us report, the four named practices and the forty-day upper limit, without supplying an unprinted prescriber. The footnote and appended comparative grade/transmitter-memory appraisal are source apparatus. Do not infer a narrator or legal expansion from another version.",
 ('tirmidhi:2769','hi'): "Read every Hindi word. Muawiya ibn Hayda narrates his three actual questions about covering, being among others and being alone, with the reported replies and the wife/bondwoman exception. Preserve every condition, exception and first/third-person source transition under the supplied dialogue; no missing participant or present instruction is added. The appended grade, Bahz-grandfather naming and father/route explanation are compiler biography/transmission context.",
 ('tirmidhi:2769','ur'): "The complete Urdu independently gives the three questions and replies, including the exact spouse/bondwoman exception, possible efforts among others and the alone question's stated response. Preserve those actual scope limits and printed grammatical forms. The compiler grade, fuller grandfather genealogy and route/father explanation remain context, rather than additional narrated statements or inferred identities.",
}
